/-!
# Relative regular equations after arbitrary base change

Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tags 0FUD and 063U, and More on Algebra, Tag 0CEP
(blueprint: lem-pur-relative-regular-equations-base-change).

The primes in the conclusion contain the specified equations, so regularity includes
nonvanishing of the final quotient. No flatness of the changing algebra is assumed.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v w

open scoped TensorProduct

namespace RingTheory.Sequence

/-- A regular sequence in a flat finitely presented algebra with flat final quotient
remains regular at every prime containing its images after arbitrary base change.
Source: Stacks Project, Divisors, Tag 0FUD, remark-relative-regular-immersion-elements,
and Tag 063U, lemma-relative-regular-immersion-flat-in-neighbourhood; More on Algebra,
Tag 0CEP (blueprint: lem-pur-relative-regular-equations-base-change).

Proof, following the six steps of the node:
1. For the empty sequence the prime localization is nonzero, proving the claim.
   Otherwise put J = (f_1, ..., f_b). The regular-sequence graded-polynomial theorem
   `lem-pur-regular-sequence-graded-polynomial` identifies (A/J)[T_1, ..., T_b]
   with gr_J(A), with each T_j mapping to the degree-one class of f_j.
2. Contract q to a prime of A and then to p in R. In the fibre F = A ⊗_R κ(p),
   this gives a prime P containing all the images of f_j. The flatness of A/J and
   `lem-pur-quasi-regular-flat-quotient-base-change` give the same polynomial
   associated-graded isomorphism for those images in F. The finite presentation
   makes F finite type over the field κ(p), hence Noetherian by
   `lem-wf-affine-noetherian-components`.
3. Localize at P. Exactness of localization
   (`lem-weil-pbc-localization-module-exact`) identifies the localized graded pieces
   with the graded pieces of F_P: powers commute with localization by multiplying
   fractions. Polynomial coefficients localize as well. The equations lie in the
   maximal ideal, so `lem-pur-quasi-regular-noetherian-local` makes them regular in F_P.
4. Spread this sequence to F[1/γ] by
   `lem-exc-regular-sequence-principal-neighbourhood`. Write γ = (a mod pA)/r,
   with r outside p. Then a is outside the contracted prime in A. Put D = A[1/a].
   Adjoining an inverse preserves finite presentation. Flatness follows by tensoring
   injections first with A and then applying exact localization. The fibre of D is
   F[1/ā], and all the specified equations form a regular sequence there.
5. Induct on the prefix length e, shrinking D = A[1/h] with h outside the contracted
   prime. Maintain that each D_i = D/(f_1, ..., f_i), i ≤ e, is flat and finitely
   presented over R, the first e multiplication maps are injective, and every
   multiplication map of the full fibre sequence is injective. Step 4 is e = 0.
   For e < b, apply `lem-pc-algebra-fibre-cartier-local` to D_e, κ(p), the induced
   prime, and the principal ideal (f_{e+1}). Its fibre is the corresponding prefix
   quotient of D ⊗_R κ(p). Fibre regularity supplies the criterion at every fibre
   prime, taking the shrinking element to be 1. The criterion supplies an element
   outside the induced prime after inverting which the ideal is generated by a
   nonzerodivisor v and its quotient is flat. Since v = t f_{e+1}, the specified
   f_{e+1} is itself a nonzerodivisor. Lift the new denominator to D and write it as
   c/h^n; replace h by hc. Exact localization preserves earlier injections and flat
   quotients. All prefix quotients remain finitely presented by adjoining their
   equations and inverse generators. This proves the inductive claim up to e = b.
6. Each short exact sequence D_{i-1} --f_i--> D_{i-1} --> D_i has flat final term.
   Apply `lem-weil-pbc-flat-quotient-tensor-injective` with N = R', then flip tensor
   factors. The tensor-quotient identification sends (d mod I) ⊗ r' to [d ⊗ r'];
   thus the images of f_j form a weakly regular sequence in D ⊗_R R'. This ring is
   (A ⊗_R R')[1/(h ⊗ 1)] by the map (a/h^n) ⊗ r' ↦ (a ⊗ r')/(h ⊗ 1)^n.
   As h ⊗ 1 is outside q, localize further at q and apply
   `lem-weil-pbc-weakly-regular-sequence-localization-prime`. The resulting ring
   is (A ⊗_R R')_q. Its final quotient has residue field κ(q), since every
   equation lies in q. This gives regularity, including the nonzero-quotient clause.
-/
theorem isRegular_localizationAtPrime_tensorProduct_of_flat_quotient
    {R : Type u} {A : Type v} {R' : Type w}
    [CommRing R] [CommRing A] [CommRing R'] [Algebra R A] [Algebra R R']
    [Module.Flat R A] [Algebra.FinitePresentation R A]
    {b : ℕ} (f : Fin b → A) (hreg : IsRegular A (List.ofFn f))
    [Module.Flat R (A ⧸ Ideal.span (Set.range f))]
    (q : Ideal (A ⊗[R] R')) [q.IsPrime]
    (hmem : ∀ j : Fin b, f j ⊗ₜ[R] (1 : R') ∈ q) :
    IsRegular (Localization.AtPrime q)
      (List.ofFn fun j : Fin b ↦
        algebraMap (A ⊗[R] R') (Localization.AtPrime q) (f j ⊗ₜ[R] (1 : R'))) := by
  sorry

end RingTheory.Sequence
